# `interface Customer { id: number }` beside a type with the same names whose `id` is an object that nobody is seen to make (a schema of a validation library: `{ id: ZodNumber }`): one set of names is one layout,
# and its field is written `number | View`. No layout stands for the view, so the type as a whole reads as anything: whoever rests a proof on it knows nothing.
# SUCH A TYPE GUARDS NOTHING, AND NOTHING RESTS ON WHAT IS ONLY EXPECTED: what else the type names is the guess: `id` a number, `owner` a Customer, `tags` a list of texts, whether the field was written guarded or expected.
# THE RED CONTROLS: the same type at a field that the compiler has established reads as anything, as before . a type that names the view alone says nothing either way . a type parameter stands for any value: nothing else is expected beside it.
